{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Put off your shoes from upon your feet.</b> The commentators were aroused by several differences between this vision and the vision of Joshua, for here it says ״שַׁל נְעָלֶיךָ״ — two in meaning, and by Joshua it says ״שַׁל נַעַלְךָ״ — one in meaning; and here it says ״כִּי הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה עוֹמֵד עָלָיו אַדְמַת קֹדֶשׁ הוּא״, and by Joshua it is not said so, but rather ״קֹדֶשׁ״ without qualification and not ״אַדְמַת קֹדֶשׁ״. Now I have seen that Rabbeinu Bachaye and other commentators explained ״שַׁל נְעָלֶיךָ״ as referring to the removal of corporeality, for Moses was more freed from materiality than Joshua; therefore I choose their path and add onyx from my own to resolve all the doubts we have mentioned. After the manner in which our Rabbis of blessed memory explained (Bava Batra 75a) ״פְּנֵי מֹשֶׁה כַּחַמָּה וִיהוֹשֻׁעַ כַּלְּבָנָה״ — for just as the moon illuminates from one side and is dark from the other, so Joshua illuminated from the side of the intellect but was dark from the side of the materiality that remained in him. But the face of Moses was like the sun, which illuminates from every side; so Moses was purified also in the material part of himself, so thoroughly that he merited horns of light on the face of his matter, for he was purified through standing on the mountain forty days without eating or drinking, and was sustained by the radiance of His Shekhinah, blessed be He. Therefore it was said to Moses ״שַׁל נְעָלֶיךָ״ — two in meaning — an allusion to the negation of materiality from both his parts: both the purification of the intellectual faculty from admixture with the materiality within him, and the purification of the material faculty itself. And therefore He said, giving a reason, ״כִּי הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה עוֹמֵד עָלָיו אַדְמַת קֹדֶשׁ״. And the name ״מָקוֹם״ alludes to the spiritual rung, and He said: the rung in which you stand is such that even the earthly part within you is holy — this is what He said: ״אַדְמַת קֹדֶשׁ הוּא״. And since you are on so great a rung, therefore I said ״אַל תִּקְרַב הֲלֹם״ to gaze at the Shekhinah, for ״לֹא יִרְאַנִי הָאָדָם וָחָי״ (Exodus 33:20), and even the angels do not draw near to Him, blessed be He, to gaze at His essence, blessed be He — all the more so a human being while he is alive in this world." "And this is what they said — that the entire house was filled with light when Moses was born — for this was a sign to him that the human house, namely his own person, would be illuminated from every side. But Joshua, who did not illuminate except from the side of the intellect alone, therefore it was said to him ״שַׁל נַעַלְךָ״ (Joshua 5:15) — one in meaning — for the materiality was removed only from the intellectual part alone. And therefore ״אַדְמַת קֹדֶשׁ״ was not mentioned in connection with him, for the earthly part within him was not sanctified, nor was it necessary to say to him ״אַל תִּקְרַב הֲלֹם״ (Exodus 3:5), nor was it necessary to prevent him from drawing near to HaShem, for in any case the material would have been an interposing barrier between him and his Elohim, so that he would not be able to gaze at the Elohim. And perhaps for this reason it says ״וַיִּקְרָא מֹשֶׁה מֹשֶׁה״ (Exodus 3:4) twice, for HaShem called to both his parts, for both of them are holy — the matter and the form." "And some say that He called to him Moses, Moses, because the exile of Israel is like a burden upon Him, blessed be He, as it were; therefore He called him with call after call, to hasten his coming and to lighten from upon Him, blessed be He, the burden — as it is written (Psalms 91:15) ״עִמּוֹ אָנֹכִי בְצָרָה״, and as it is written (Exodus 3:7) ״כִּי יָדַעְתִּי אֶת מַכְאֹבָיו״ — ״כִּי יָדַעְתִּי״, that is, He feels their pain, as it were."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:14.987827138Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}